              "inline_data": null,
              "text": "The official and most secure way to make a federal tax payment online is through the **Internal Revenue Service (IRS) website**. \n\nDepending on your situation, you can pay directly from your bank account (for free), use a credit or debit card (for a fee), or schedule payments through an online account. \n\n---\n\n### 1. IRS Direct Pay (Best Overall for Individuals)\nIf you want to make a quick personal tax payment straight from your checking or savings account, **IRS Direct Pay** is the fastest and most convenient method.\n* **Cost:** Free.\n* **No Account Required:** You do not need to log in or create an IRS account to use it. \n* **Verification:** The system will verify your identity using details from a tax return filed in a prior year (up to 6 years ago).\n* **What you can pay:** Tax return balances, quarterly estimated taxes, and filing extensions (Form 4868).\n* **Where to go:** [irs.gov/directpay](https://www.irs.gov/payments/direct-pay)\n\n### 2. IRS Online Account (Best for Managing Balance & History)\nCreating or logging into your personal IRS Online Account gives you full control over your tax records and payments.\n* **Cost:** Free.\n* **Verification:** Requires logging in securely (typically via an ID.me account).\n* **Benefits:** \n  * View your overall balance, payment history, and past tax records.\n  * Schedule payments up to 365 days in advance.\n  * Set up and monitor payment plans.\n* **Where to go:** [irs.gov/account](https://www.irs.gov/payments/your-online-account)\n\n### 3. Credit Card, Debit Card, or Digital Wallet (Fastest)\nIf you want to pay using a debit card, credit card, or digital wallet (like PayPal or Click to Pay), the IRS uses secure, approved third-party payment processors.\n* **Cost:** The IRS does not charge a fee, but **the processing partners do**:\n  * **Debit cards:** Flat fee of approximately $2.00 to $2.50 per transaction.\n  * **Credit cards:** Percentage fee of approximately 1.75% to 1.85% of your payment amount.\n* **Where to go:** You can choose from the IRS-approved processors (such as Pay1040, ACI Payments, or Link2Gov) on the official IRS website: [irs.gov/paybycard](https://www.irs.gov/payments/pay-your-taxes-by-debit-or-credit-card).\n\n### 4. Electronic Funds Withdrawal (While Filing)\nIf you are e-filing your current-year tax return using software (like TurboTax, H&R Block, etc.) or through a professional tax preparer, you can authorize an **Electronic Funds Withdrawal (EFW)**.\n* **Cost:** Free.\n* **How it works:** You input your bank routing and account numbers directly into the tax software, and the payment is automatically debited when the return is processed.\n\n### 5. Business Payments (EFTPS & Business Tax Account)\nIf you are paying corporate, partnership, or employment taxes:\n* **EFTPS (Electronic Federal Tax Payment System):** This is the primary electronic portal for business and federal tax deposit payments. It requires setting up an account and waiting for a PIN to arrive in the mail.\n  * *Note for Individuals:* Individuals can no longer create *new* EFTPS accounts. If you already have an active EFTPS account as an individual, you can still use it, but the IRS encourages individuals to transition to using their IRS Online Account.\n* **Business Tax Account:** You can also register for an IRS Business Tax Account to view balances and make federal tax deposits online.\n* **Where to go:** [eftps.gov](https://www.eftps.gov/eftps/) or [irs.gov/businessaccount](https://www.irs.gov/businesses/business-tax-account)\n\n---\n\n### Can't pay your taxes in full?\nIf you are unable to pay the entire balance you owe, you should still file your return on time to avoid late-filing penalties. You can then apply online for an **IRS Payment Plan (Installment Agreement)** to pay your balance over time (up to 72 months). \n* **Where to apply:** [irs.gov/paymentplan](https://www.irs.gov/payments/online-payment-agreement-application)\n\n\u26a0\ufe0f **Security Warning:** *Always ensure that you are on a genuine `.gov` website (such as `irs.gov` or `eftps.gov`) before entering your Social Security Number, bank account details, or credit card information. The IRS will never text, email, or message you out of the blue asking for immediate payment info.*",
              "thought": null,
